"text": "On a pipe, the two are basically the same point: the inside bottom of the pipe, where the water flows. Invert is the elevation at that low point, and flowline is the line along the inverts [5][6].\n- Plan elevations for gravity pipe are almost always inverts, and crews often use \"flowline\" to mean the same thing [5].\n- On a stake, \"C 8.42 FL\" means cut 8.42 ft from the hub down to the flowline (invert) [1].\n- Don't confuse either with BOP (outside bottom) or TOP (outside top). Bottom of pipe is the invert minus the wall thickness [5].",
